James encountered an issue in Julia when a variable defined in `one.jl` was not accessible in a function from `two.jl` due to scope rules. I explained that `include` pastes the file content into the current module's namespace, which may lead to `UndefVarError`. The problem arose because `two.jl` created a module that did not recognize `x` from `Main`, leading to errors in accessing the variable. I offered solutions including redefining `x` within the module, explicitly referring to `Main.x`, or passing `x` as an argument. James realized the source of the problem was the module structure in `two.jl` and sought further assistance in arranging the files appropriately.
